{% extends "base.njk" %}

{% block content %}

<div class="page-header">
  <h1>About Hamaki</h1>
  <p>Two lecturers, one scheduling problem, and a Japanese hammock.</p>
</div>

{# ===== MISSION ===== #}
<section>
  <div class="section-wrapper narrow">
    <div class="section-header center">
      <div class="section-label">Our mission</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Scheduling should feel like a hammock</h2>
    </div>

    <p style="font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.85;color:var(--text-body);text-align:center;max-width:620px;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;">
      ハンモック — <em>hanmokku</em> — is the Japanese word for hammock.
      That's the feeling Hamaki is built around: once your booking page is set up,
      you lean back while everything arranges itself.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.85;color:var(--text-muted);text-align:center;max-width:580px;margin:0 auto;">
      No more reply-all email threads. No sharing your calendar with a cloud service.
      Just a link, a page, and people who book themselves in.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ===== TEAM ===== #}
<section class="section-alt">
  <div class="section-wrapper">
    <div class="section-header center">
      <div class="section-label">The makers</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Meet Raymond &amp; Tjerk</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">
        Both lecturers at NHL Stenden University of Applied Sciences,
        both tired of scheduling meetings by email.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="team-grid">

      <div class="team-card">
        <div class="team-avatar">R</div>
        <div class="team-name">Raymond van Dongelen</div>
        <div class="team-role">Developer &amp; Lecturer</div>
        <p class="team-bio">
          Raymond teaches at the Communication &amp; Multimedia Design bachelor's programme (CMDLWD)
          at NHL Stenden. He also makes macOS apps because the ones he needed didn't exist yet.
          Hamaki is the best proof of that.
          He writes about design and technology at raymond.frl.
        </p>
        <div class="team-links">
          <a href="https://raymond.frl" target="_blank" class="team-link">
            <i class="fas fa-globe"></i> raymond.frl
          </a>
          <a href="https://apps.apple.com/nl/developer/raymond-van-dongelen/id926761221?l=en-GB&platform=mac"
             target="_blank" class="team-link">
            <i class="fab fa-apple"></i> App Store
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="team-card">
        <div class="team-avatar">T</div>
        <div class="team-name">Tjerk Dijkstra</div>
        <div class="team-role">Designer &amp; Lecturer · Potloodgum</div>
        <p class="team-bio">
          Tjerk (known online as Potloodgum) teaches alongside Raymond at NHL Stenden's
          CMDLWD programme. He brings the design sensibility to Hamaki — the sharp eye
          that makes the interface feel considered and calm, not cluttered.
          You'll find him on Mastodon at mastodon.design.
        </p>
        <div class="team-links">
          <a href="https://mastodon.design/@potloodgum" target="_blank" class="team-link">
            <i class="fab fa-mastodon"></i> Mastodon
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ===== ORIGIN STORY ===== #}
<section>
  <div class="section-wrapper narrow">
    <div class="section-header center">
      <div class="section-label">The origin</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Why we built it</h2>
    </div>

    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.5rem;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;padding:1.75rem 1.5rem;">
        <div class="feature-icon" style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><i class="fas fa-calendar-alt"></i></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--text-dark);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:1.7;margin:0;">
          At NHL Stenden's CMDLWD bachelor's programme, students need regular one-on-one meetings
          with their lecturers — feedback sessions, project reviews, portfolio critiques.
          Organising these by email meant an endless ping-pong of "are you free Thursday at 2?"
          "No, what about 3?" "I have a meeting, how about Friday?"
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;padding:1.75rem 1.5rem;">
        <div class="feature-icon" style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--text-dark);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The idea</h3>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:1.7;margin:0;">
          Online scheduling tools exist, but they all require sharing your calendar with a cloud
          service, signing up for yet another account, or both. Raymond and Tjerk wanted something
          that worked with their existing Mac and Calendar.app — private, local, and actually fun to use.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;padding:1.75rem 1.5rem;">
        <div class="feature-icon" style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><i class="fas fa-hammer"></i></div>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--text-dark);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The result</h3>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;color:var(--text-muted);line-height:1.7;margin:0;">
          Hamaki launched in 2024 and has been shipped continuously since. From basic booking pages
          to waiting lists, AI integration, Spotlight search, and a CLI — it keeps growing because
          Raymond and Tjerk use it themselves every day. When something is annoying, they fix it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ===== VALUES ===== #}
<section class="section-alt">
  <div class="section-wrapper">
    <div class="section-header center">
      <div class="section-label">What we believe</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Our principles</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="features-grid">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon"><i class="fas fa-home"></i></div>
        <h3>Your data, your Mac</h3>
        <p>Hamaki processes everything locally. Your schedule doesn't get synced to our servers, because we don't have any.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon"><i class="fas fa-apple-alt"></i></div>
        <h3>A real Mac app</h3>
        <p>Not a web app wrapped in Electron. Hamaki uses native macOS APIs, respects system settings, and lives in the menu bar where it belongs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-icon"><i class="fas fa-tools"></i></div>
        <h3>Built by users</h3>
        <p>Every feature in Hamaki exists because Raymond or Tjerk needed it. No roadmaps driven by investor decks. Just real problems, solved well.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{# ===== CTA ===== #}
<section class="cta-banner">
  <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
  <p>Free to start. One booking calendar, unlimited spots, no account needed.</p>
  <a href="https://apps.apple.com/nl/app/hamaki/id1627733431?l=en-GB&mt=12"
     class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" style="font-size:1.05rem;padding:0.85rem 1.75rem;">
    <i class="fab fa-apple"></i> Download on Mac App Store
  </a>
</section>

{% endblock %}
